\section{Discussion and future work}

The strength of the resolution is hypothesis-minimality: the fusion theorem
needs only integer-valued non-negative counts, an expectation realized as a
bounded product, and enough independent hurdles --- nothing about alien
behavior. The conclusion is conditional and conservative.

The first moment method is one half of a dichotomy; its mirror is the
Paley--Zygmund second-moment inequality: if $\E[X] > 1$ and
$\E[X^2] \le C\,\E[X]^2$, then $\Pr[X \ge 1] \ge 1/C > 0$.

\paragraph{Future directions.}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.4em]
  \item \textbf{C1 (tightness).} Characterize equality in
  Theorem~\ref{thm:tail}: $w(Z) = 1-\E[X]$ iff $X$ is $\{0,1\}$-valued on its
  support.
  \item \textbf{C2 (second moment).} Prove the Paley--Zygmund companion over
  finite weighted $\Q$-spaces via Cauchy--Schwarz on a finite set.
  \item \textbf{C3 (dimension robustness).} Any $\ge 11$ independent hurdles
  each $\le 1/10$ with $N \le 10^{10}$ force $\E < 1$ regardless of precise
  per-hurdle values.
  \item \textbf{C4 (resonant listening).} Quantify the resonant listening
  window, relating detection probability to $\gcdop$ of periods via Fibonacci
  strong divisibility.
\end{itemize}

\section{Conclusion}

Fermi's question presupposes that abundance is the default and silence the
surprise. The mathematics reverses the presumption. With honest, conservative
priors the expected number of communicating civilizations is at most $0.1$, and
the first moment method --- the pigeonhole principle made quantitative ---
guarantees that the probability of an empty cosmos is at least $0.9$. The Great
Silence is not a paradox; it is the most probable observation, forced by
counting alone.

